# DNA-AND content · solar systems
# researched: exoplanet architecture reviews (resonant chains: TRAPPIST-1, TOI-178); NASA Solar System overviews
# Real seeds for the solar-system generator: kinds of systems, real examples,
# the zones a system is built from, and the rules.
# Distances in au (1 au = the Earth–Sun distance). Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r05-universe.md

# ── Kinds of systems (real) ──

# How a system is laid out (the usual one, sun-like, first; a kind asked for gives only what differs): planets: how many
#   worlds; first-orbit-au: the first world's orbit in AU, times a value in first-orbit-spread; scales-with-star: moved out
#   by the square root of the star's brightness (yes) or not; spacing: each orbit over the one before (the Sun's worlds
#   about 1.4 to 2); share-in-universe: how often a system in the universe is of this kind.
@system sun-like
name: Sun-like system
real: yes
star: g-type or k-type
shape: small rocky planets close in, gas and ice giants further out, belts and an icy outer shell
example: the Solar System
planets: 3..10
first-orbit-au: 0.3
first-orbit-spread: 0.6..1.4
scales-with-star: yes
spacing: 1.45..1.9

@system compact
name: Compact system
real: yes
star: m-type
shape: many rocky planets packed tightly close to a dim star
example: TRAPPIST-1
planets: 6..8
first-orbit-au: 0.012
first-orbit-spread: 0.8..1.2
scales-with-star: no
spacing: 1.25..1.4

@system hot-jupiter
name: Hot-Jupiter system
real: yes
shape: a gas giant orbiting very close to its star, usually with few close neighbors
example: 51 Pegasi
planets: 1..3
share-in-universe: 0.1

@system crowded
name: Crowded system
real: yes
shape: many planets of every size
example: Kepler-90

@system circumbinary
name: Circumbinary system
real: yes
star: two stars close together
shape: planets orbiting both stars at once
example: Kepler-16

@system wide-binary
name: Wide binary system
real: yes
star: two stars far apart
shape: each star with its own planets
example: Alpha Centauri

@system triple
name: Triple-star system
real: yes
star: three stars
shape: planets around one star, the others far off
example: Alpha Centauri (with Proxima Centauri)

@system red-dwarf
name: Red-dwarf system
real: yes
star: m-type
shape: planets close in, often locked with one face to the star
example: Proxima Centauri

@system giant-free
name: Giant-free system
real: yes
shape: only small rocky planets

@system young
name: Young system
real: yes
shape: a star still wrapped in its dust and gas disk, planets still forming

@system debris
name: Debris-disk system
real: yes
shape: a grown star with a wide belt of dust and rock left over

@system white-dwarf
name: White-dwarf system
real: yes
star: white dwarf
shape: the survivors of a dead Sun-like star, often with rubble falling in

@system pulsar
name: Pulsar system
real: yes
star: pulsar
shape: planets circling a spinning dead star
example: PSR B1257+12, home of the first planets ever found outside the Solar System

@system lone-planet
name: Starless planet
real: yes
shape: a rogue planet with no star, sometimes with moons

# ── Real examples ──

@example solar-system
name: The Solar System
real: yes
star: the Sun
planets: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune
dwarf-planets: Ceres, Pluto, Eris, Haumea, Makemake
belts: asteroid belt, Kuiper belt, Oort cloud

@example trappist-1
name: TRAPPIST-1
real: yes
star: m-type red dwarf
planets: 7 rocky planets, several in the habitable zone

@example proxima-centauri
name: Proxima Centauri
real: yes
star: m-type red dwarf, the closest star to the Sun
planets: at least one planet in the habitable zone

@example 51-pegasi
name: 51 Pegasi
real: yes
star: g-type
planets: 51 Pegasi b, the first planet found around a Sun-like star, a hot Jupiter

# ── Zones a system is built from ──

@zone inner-rocky
name: Inner rocky zone
real: yes
says: close to the star, too hot for ice; small rocky planets form here

@zone habitable
name: Habitable zone
real: yes
says: the band where liquid water can last on a planet's surface; its distance depends on how bright the star is
solar-system-au: about 0.95..1.4

@zone frost-line
name: Frost line
real: yes
says: the distance where ice can form; beyond it, giant planets can grow
solar-system-au: about 2.7

@zone asteroid-belt
name: Asteroid belt
real: yes
says: a ring of rocks between the rocky planets and the giants
solar-system-au: about 2.2..3.2

@zone giant
name: Giant zone
real: yes
says: gas giants and ice giants, each with many moons and often rings

@zone kuiper-belt
name: Kuiper belt
real: yes
says: icy bodies and dwarf planets beyond the last giant
solar-system-au: about 30..50

@zone scattered-disk
name: Scattered disk
real: yes
says: icy bodies thrown into long, tilted orbits beyond the Kuiper belt

@zone oort-cloud
name: Oort cloud
real: yes
says: a huge round shell of comets far out around the star, reaching out a light-year or more

@zone trojans
name: Trojan points
real: yes
says: spots ahead of and behind a planet in its orbit where rocks collect

@zone rings
name: Ring zone
real: yes
says: close to a giant planet, where moons can't hold together and rings form

@system resonant-chain
name: Resonant-chain system
real: yes
shape: planets whose orbits lock together in a steady beat, each lap timed to the next
example: TRAPPIST-1, TOI-178

@zone detached
name: Detached objects
real: yes
says: icy bodies on huge lonely orbits beyond the Kuiper belt
example: Sedna

@zone heliosphere
name: Heliosphere
real: yes
says: the bubble the star's wind blows around the whole system; its edge is where interstellar space begins

@rule system-resonance
real: rule
says: young planets often drift into locked resonant chains; most chains later break apart

@rule system-hill-sphere
real: rule
says: a moon can only stay with a planet inside that planet's sphere of pull; too far out and the star takes it

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule system-seed
real: rule
says: every system is generated live from the world seed; nothing is pre-built

@rule system-star-first
real: rule
says: the star is picked first; its type sets where the habitable zone and frost line fall

@rule system-zones
real: rule
says: rocky planets mostly form inside the frost line, giants outside it, belts sit in the gaps, and an Oort cloud surrounds it all

@rule system-migration
real: rule
says: giant planets can wander inward after forming, making hot Jupiters and scattering smaller planets and comets

@rule system-moons
real: rule
says: giants collect many moons; rocky planets have few or none; big impacts can make a large moon

@rule system-comets-fall
real: rule
says: comets from the Oort cloud and Kuiper belt sometimes fall inward, and some hit planets

@rule system-multiple-stars
real: rule
says: in two- and three-star systems, planets orbit one star or circle the whole group, never in between

@rule system-life-zone
real: rule
says: life is most likely in the habitable zone, but ice-shell moons and other strange worlds can hold life too

@rule system-age
real: rule
says: young systems still have dust disks and forming planets; old systems have settled orbits

@rule system-name
real: rule
says: only our own system is the Solar System and only our own star is the Sun; generated ones get generated names
